> Can you summarize this?
> ===
> Bangladesh Climate Development Partnership (BCDP): An Evolving Country Platform 
> 
> Shah Abdul Saadi, PhD
> 
> 1. Background 
> 
> The grand societal challenge of climate change is inherently cross-sectoral, multi-scalar, and evolving, requiring coordinated, coherent, and adaptive governance responses at scale and speed across the local, national, and international levels.
> 
> The challenges of climate change are inherently cross-sectoral, multi-scalar, and dynamic, requiring coordinated, coherent governance responses at local, national, and international levels. Policy scholars emphasise the critical importance of synergy and coherence—both horizontal (across sectors, actors, and institutions) and vertical (across governance levels)—in planning, designing, and implementing climate change adaptation and mitigation policies and actions (Weitz et al., 2015; Karlsson-Vinkhuyzen et al., 2017; Nilsson et al., 2012; OECD, 2015). 
> 
> Bangladesh is internationally recognised for its proactive stance on climate adaptation and disaster risk reduction, underpinned by short-, medium-, and long-term climate policies and action plans, including the Climate Change Strategy and Action Plan (BCCSAP) in 2008, the National Adaptation Plan 2023-2050, the third Nationally Determined Contributions (NDC 3.0), the Bangladesh Climate Prosperity Plan: Decade 2030 (Draft), the National Biodiversity Strategy and Action Plan (NBSAP) 2023-2030, and the National Land Degradation Neutrality (NLDN) Targets 2018. However, these efforts often remain severely underfunded and, as a result, underimplemented, mainly due to a lack of both vertical and horizontal coherence, which hampers resource mobilisation and deployment at scale and speed (Islam and Nur, 2020; Dewan et al., 2021). Just to mention, Bangladesh’s current annual climate finance deficit is estimated at 29.29 USD. In this context, despite challenges and uncertainties, the Government of Bangladesh (GoB) established the Bangladesh Climate Development Partnership (BCDP) in 2023 to improve coherence and synergy in climate governance, thereby mobilising additional and innovative climate finance to advance climate-resilient development and support the green economic transition.
> 
>  
> 
> Fig. 1. BCDP’s organisational architecture
> 
> 
> 2. Organisational Architecture of BCDP 
> BCDP comprises a high-powered Board and four Working Groups. The BCDP Board is headed by the Principal Secretary to the Prime Minister and is intended to function as a super-ministerial body to ensure better and more effective collaboration (coordination and cooperation) among a diverse mix of actors with roles and interests in climate policies, actions and finances. The BCDP Board operates through four independent yet interdependent Working Groups. 
> 
> Working Group 1, headed by the Secretary of the Economic Relations Division (ERD) of the Ministry of Finance (MoF), coordinates the mobilisation of external finance. Working Group 2, headed by the Secretary of the Finance Division (FD) of the Ministry of Finance (MoF), facilitates the mobilisation of internal financing to implement climate projects. Working Group 3, headed by the Secretary of the Ministry of Forest, Environment, and Climate Change, is mandated to develop institutional capacity for advanced climate change interventions. Finally, Working Group 4, headed by the Secretary of the Planning Division, provides project readiness support. These four working groups coordinate and collaborate to design viable, investible climate projects that can mobilise finance at scale and speed.       
> 
> 
> 3. A whole-of-society approach – cornerstone of BCDP
> Climate change adaptation and mitigation interventions constitute a complex, systemic challenge that transcends government capacity and sectoral boundaries. According to the IPCC (2022), climate change affects all societal dimensions—economy, health, food security, infrastructure, and social equity—and therefore no single actor or sector can address its risks and opportunities alone (IPCC, 2022). The whole-of-society approach advocates mobilising not only government agencies but also the private sector, civil society, academia, communities, and individuals to foster broader ownership, resource pooling, innovation, and legitimacy for climate responses (Pelling et al., 2015; Moser, 2016; Archer et al., 2017; UNDP, 2020). This approach is the cornerstone of BCDP. To implement this approach, BCDP is guided by four principles: Synergy, Scale & Speed, and Sustainability (4S Principles). 
> 
> BCDP aims to improve synergy by ensuring coherence across actor constituencies, policy domains, and financial provisions. BCDP and its Working Groups are composed of actors from relevant government ministries and departments, development partners (e.g., the World Bank and ADB), the private sector, civil society organisations, and academia. For example, beyond public-sector actors and development partner (i.e., ADB), Working Group 2 (External Climate Finance) includes Ms Vidiya Amrit Khan, Vice President, BGMEA (Private Sector), Ms Farha Kabir Country Director, ActionAid, Bangladesh (CSO: Civil Society Organisation), and Ms Saima Ahmed, PhD, Assistant Professor, Dept. of International Relations, University of Dhaka. Each Working Group has representation in other Working Groups, which serves as an integrating mechanism between climate projects, finance (internal and external, public and private), country ownership and climate policy alignment. An interactive website has been launched, which, inter alia, serves as a platform for Working Groups to share their data. Thus, by bringing together key actors on a single country platform, BCDP fosters synergy among climate policies, actions and financing, thereby reducing siloing, fragmentation and overlap and enhancing complementarity.  
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> 
> Given Bangladesh’s intensifying climate risks from cyclones, floods, droughts, sea-level rise and salinity intrusion, the scale (the breadth and magnitude of interventions) and speed (the rapidity of implementation and finance deployment) of climate action are not just desirable—they are critical to protecting lives, livelihoods, and development gains. (IPCC, 2022; Huq and Rabbani, 2018; Ayers et al., 2014). Bangladesh’s extreme climate vulnerabilities demand a correspondingly large-scale and urgent response, as piecemeal or slow interventions risk leaving millions unprotected and undermining resilience-building (Huq et al., 2018; Ayers et al., 2014). The high-powered, action-focused BCDP is committed to facilitating the design and implementation of climate actions proportionate to the need and urgency by coordinating with key actors to overcome bureaucratic formalities and bottlenecks. The BCDP has begun fulfilling this promise. For example, following the directive from the first meeting of the BCDP Board, held on 18 September 2025, four climate projects were approved for internal financing, marking the fastest financing decision in the Bangladesh planning process. Furthermore, the second meeting of Working Group 2 (External Climate Finance), held on 7th December 2025, recommended the desirable scale of river restoration and municipal waste management projects and their fast-tracking to accelerate the mobilisation of external financial and technical support.  
> 
> Sustainability in climate action extends beyond individual one-off projects, necessitating a shift towards enduring institutional processes and business models that embed climate considerations into the fabric of governance (e.g., sectoral development plans, fiscal policies, planning and budgeting), and entrepreneurship (Ayers et al., 2014; Runhaar et al., 2018; Owen et al., 2018; Lüdeke-Freund et al., 2017; Berrang-Ford et al., 2021; Agrawal et al., 2012). BCDP has been established under statutory regulations, with the Ministry of Environment, Forest, and Climate Change (MoEFCC) serving as its Secretariat. The regulatory and organisational framework of BCDP aims to anchor climate interventions in robust, adaptive institutions to enable them to sustain and scale. 
> 
> The BCDP Board, through itself and its four Working Groups, brings together key climate-relevant line ministries – such as finance, agriculture, fisheries & livestock, local government, health, power, energy, transportation, and infrastructure – under a single superior coordinating authority to integrate climate action into development planning, budgeting, sectoral policies, and organisational strategies—rather than treating it as a stand-alone issue—enhancing sustainability and system-wide impact. 
> 
> BCDP subscribes to the view that sustainable business models—those that align profit motives with climate resilience, mitigation, or adaptation—are key to mobilizing private finance and maintaining momentum after development partners withdraw or public funding ends (Owen et al., 2018; Lüdeke-Freund et al., 2017). This approach aims to reduce reliance on grants, contain the rising debt burden, and leverage private finance to accelerate climate action. As an important progress, the International Climate Finance Cell (ICFC) of the Economic Relations Division (which leads the BCDP Working Group 2) has started collaborating with private sector actors (e.g., BGMEA), banks (e.g., City Bank), non-banking financial and service entities (e.g., IWM, PKSF, IDCOL, SME Foundation, Adorsho PraniSheba Limited), regulatory actors (e.g., BSEC and Bangladesh Bank), relevant development partners (UNDP, UNCDF, UNIDO), civil society organisations (CSOs), and entrepreneurs to introduce green bonds, orange bonds, insurance (under the Global Shield against Climate Risk initiative), and to leverage market-based financing instruments such as catalytic concessional finance (e.g., soft loans, seed money), equity, guarantees, and the PPP model of investment to embed climate actions within a sustainable business logic, incentivising entrepreneurship, innovation and longevity.          
> 
> Recognising that sustainable climate action requires systems that learn and adapt over time, BCDP Working Group 3 is entirely dedicated to capacity development to improve project readiness and update climate plans and strategies based on monitoring, evaluation, and changing conditions. Apart from Working Group 3, other groups also contribute to thematic capacity development, e.g., climate negotiations and climate finance. For instance, Working Group 2 (External Climate Finance), at its second meeting on 7 December 2025, approved an Action Plan for 2026, setting a target to organise five training programmes to improve the climate literacy, climate finance knowledge and climate negotiation skills of public and private sector actors, professionals from CSOs, media and academia as well as youth climate enthusiasts. ADB has committed to providing financial assistance, while ICFC will provide technical support in organising these training programmes. It is noteworthy that ICFC, which provides secretarial support to the BCDP Working Group 2 (External Climate Finance), organised eight similar training programmes in 2025 to operationalise BCDP as an effective country platform. 
> 
> Conclusion: 
> BCDP, as an evolving country platform, is working to deliver its statutory mandate by (i) translating country commitments and ambitions 1 (e.g., NAP and NDC) into sectoral priorities, policies, budgeting, and tangible, climate-resilient investment pipelines; (ii) coordinating stakeholder engagement across line ministries and government departments, multilateral/bilateral development partners, the private sector, and civil society actors to foster alignment and effective collaboration; (iii) catalysing financing at scale and speed by engaging various sources of finance (public, private, official development assistance, international climate finance, philanthropies), structuring a viable financing model, and providing strategic support to tackle barriers to climate investment and resource deployment; and (iv) capacitating key actors (e.g., line ministries, departments, and other associated actors such as those from the private sector and CSOs) to prepare viable and investible climate project pipelines, alongside their implementation, monitoring, evaluation, and reporting. ICFC has organised the EcoSynergy Initiative to design a climate-smart agriculture project involving FAO, line ministries and departments of the government, the private sector and CSO actors, demonstrating the application of all four processes. This project idea will be pitched through BCDP to mobilise internal and external financing, leveraging innovative funding instruments and modalities.
> 
> BCDP has been established to address the escalating climate financing gaps, ensuring that climate policies and plans, such as NAP and NDC, do not remain on paper as mere rhetoric. It is believed that BCDP, as an institutional mechanism, can foster partnerships and leverage the comparative advantages of different actors—government, development partners, the private sector, and civil society—creating opportunities for co-financing, joint programming, and innovation. However, it remains to be seen how BCDP evolves into an effective country platform that fosters coherent and synergistic climate governance to design and implement sustainable climate actions at scale and speed.  
